sections.body:
Instead of passing a plain value to the setter, you can pass a *function* -- React calls it with the most up-to-date pending value for that state and uses whatever it returns as the new value:

```jsx
setCount((c) => c + 1);
```

This matters the moment you call the setter for the same state more than once within a single event handler, before a re-render has happened in between:

```jsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount(count + 1);
  setCount(count + 1);
}
```

Both calls here read the exact same `count` from this render's closure (say, 0), so both are really "set state to 1" -- React doesn't treat them as two separate "add one" operations; the net effect after handleClick finishes is 1, not 2, no matter how many times you repeat that same-render `count + 1` call. Functional updates fix this precisely because each one operates on the *result of the previous update already queued*, not on the render-time snapshot:

```jsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount((c) => c + 1);
  setCount((c) => c + 1);
}
```

The first functional update receives 0 and produces 1; the second receives that just-produced 1 (not the original render-time 0) and produces 2. This is the concrete, common answer to 'why doesn't clicking this button once increment by 2 the way I expected' -- and it's the reason functional updates exist at all, rather than being a stylistic alternative.

```jsx
function DelayedCount() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  function handleLogLater() {
    setTimeout(() => {
      console.log("count is", count);
    }, 2000);
  }

  function increment() {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={increment}>+1</button>
      <button onClick={handleLogLater}>Log in 2s</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

Suppose a user clicks "+1" once (count becomes 1 after the re-render), then clicks "Log in 2s" (scheduling a timeout whose callback closes over count as it is *right now*, which is 1), then clicks "+1" twice more in the next second (count becomes 3). Two seconds after "Log in 2s" was clicked, the console prints `count is 1` -- not 3. The scheduled callback is a specific function value, permanently tied to the render during which it was created; the two later `+1` clicks each produce their own new render, with their own new `increment` function and their own freshly closed-over count value, but none of that reaches back and changes the timeout callback that's already scheduled and simply waiting to fire. Meanwhile, the screen has correctly been showing "count: 3" the whole time -- it's specifically the frozen closure inside the timeout that's out of date, not the component's actual rendered state.

When the stale value is being used to *compute a new piece of state* (not just read for display or logging), a functional update fixes the staleness, because it defers to whatever the latest queued value actually is at the moment the update is applied, rather than to whatever was closed over when the callback was created:

```jsx
setTimeout(() => {
  setCount((c) => c + 1); // always increments from the truly current value
}, 2000);
```

But functional updates only help when you're *producing a new state value* -- they don't help when you need to simply read the absolute latest value of some state from inside an already-stale closure without setting anything (for a log statement, a comparison, or passing to some other API). Functional updates aren't a general 'give me the live value' mechanism; they're specifically for computing a new value from whatever the current one turns out to be. For that read-only 'I just need the latest value, right now, inside this stale callback' case, the standard escape hatch is a **ref** kept manually in sync with the state -- covered in the next concept -- since a ref's `.current` is always read fresh through the same persistent object, rather than through a snapshot captured at closure-creation time.

