```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  // effect body
}, [dep1, dep2]);
```

On every render, React compares each dependency to the value it had on the *previous* render; the effect only re-runs if at least one of them changed. This array must honestly list every reactive value -- state, props, or anything derived from them -- that the effect body actually reads. Leaving one out isn't a harmless optimization; it's a real bug, and specifically a stale-closure bug of the exact same kind covered for useState: the effect function itself is a closure, created fresh on whichever render last caused it to run, and if a value it reads isn't listed as a dependency, the effect has no reason to run again when that value changes -- so it keeps operating on the old, closed-over value from whenever it last ran.

This is exactly why `eslint-plugin-react-hooks`'s exhaustive-deps rule exists and flags this: it's catching real, hard-to-notice bugs (an effect quietly using stale data), not enforcing a style preference. Silencing the warning with an eslint-disable comment, without actually fixing the underlying issue, doesn't resolve anything -- it just hides the warning while the bug remains. Two special cases are worth knowing: an empty array `[]` means 'run once, after the very first render, and never again'; omitting the array argument entirely means 'run after every single render,' which is rarely the intended behavior and usually a mistake rather than a deliberate choice.

If the function passed to `useEffect` returns another function, React treats that returned function as **cleanup**. It runs right before the effect runs again (for a newer set of dependency values) and once more when the component unmounts entirely:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  function handleResize() {
    setWidth(window.innerWidth);
  }
  window.addEventListener("resize", handleResize);

  return () => {
    window.removeEventListener("resize", handleResize);
  };
}, []);
```

This matters enormously for anything that would otherwise leak or duplicate. A subscription that's never unsubscribed keeps invoking a callback tied to a component that may have already re-run its effect (or unmounted) -- calling into stale closures, or updating state for a component instance that no longer logically exists. An interval or timeout that's never cleared keeps firing indefinitely. And an effect that runs more than once without cleanup in between (because its dependencies changed) can quietly accumulate multiple simultaneous subscriptions or timers, instead of always having exactly one active at a time -- so a resize handler that should fire once per resize might end up firing several times, once per leftover, uncleaned listener.

```jsx
function BuggyCounter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }, [count]);

  return <p>{count}</p>;
}
```

This effect depends on `count` and unconditionally sets a new `count` on every single run. Trace it: render (count = 0) -> effect runs because it's the first run -> setCount(1) -> re-render (count = 1) -> count changed, so the effect runs again -> setCount(2) -> re-render (count = 2) -> ... forever, pegging the CPU and never settling. The effect is, structurally, exactly the condition that causes it to run again, every single time it runs.

The fix is one of a few things: don't depend on a value you're unconditionally overwriting from inside the very same effect (often achievable with a functional update if you need the *previous* value without needing to list it as a dependency); add a genuine conditional guard so the effect only calls setState when something has changed for an unrelated reason; or -- often the right call -- recognize that the logic doesn't need an effect at all. A great deal of 'derived state' (a value computed from other state or props) doesn't belong in a useEffect-plus-setState pair; it can simply be calculated directly in the component body during render, with no effect, no extra state variable, and no risk of this loop in the first place.

```jsx
function UserProfile({ userId }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let isCurrent = true;

    fetchUser(userId).then((data) => {
      if (isCurrent) setUser(data);
    });

    return () => {
      isCurrent = false;
    };
  }, [userId]);

  return user ? <p>{user.name}</p> : <p>Loading...</p>;
}
```

This effect correctly lists `userId` as its only dependency, since that's the only reactive value it reads, and re-fetches whenever it changes. The cleanup function doesn't cancel the actual network request, but it does something just as important: it flips a flag so that if `userId` changes again *before* the current fetch resolves (a real race condition -- the user might click between two profiles quickly), the stale response is simply ignored instead of being applied to state for a userId that's no longer current. This pattern -- dependency array matching exactly what's read, plus a cleanup guarding against a stale, late-arriving result -- is close to the shape of most real-world data-fetching effects.

