```jsx
function AutoFocusInput() {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    inputRef.current.focus();
  }, []);

  return <input ref={inputRef} placeholder="Focused automatically" />;
}
```

Attaching `ref={inputRef}` to a JSX element tells React to set `inputRef.current` to the actual browser DOM node once it exists (right after that render is committed). This is an escape hatch to real, imperative DOM APIs that JSX has no declarative prop for -- calling `.focus()`, `.scrollIntoView()`, measuring an element's size with `.getBoundingClientRect()`, or integrating a non-React library that expects a raw DOM node to attach itself to. `inputRef.current` starts out `null` (before the element exists) and only becomes the real node once React has mounted it, which is exactly why the `.focus()` call above happens inside a useEffect -- effects run after the DOM has actually been updated for that render, so the node is guaranteed to exist by the time the effect body runs.

