sections.body:
A component function runs again, from the top, on every render. Any object literal, array literal, or function definition written directly inside that function body is therefore a brand-new value every single time, even when it looks identical to what the previous render produced:

```jsx
function Parent() {
  const config = { color: "blue" }; // a genuinely new object, every render
  return <Child config={config} />;
}
```

`config` is a different object reference on every render of Parent, even though `{ color: "blue" }` reads identically each time. This matters anywhere something downstream cares about reference identity rather than content -- most importantly, a dependency array (which compares references) or a component wrapped in `React.memo` (which shallow-compares props by reference).

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback((id) => {
  doSomething(id);
}, [doSomething]);
```

`useCallback(fn, deps)` is defined to behave identically to `useMemo(() => fn, deps)` -- it exists purely as a slightly clearer, dedicated name for the extremely common specific case of memoizing a *function reference*, rather than being a fundamentally different mechanism. There's nothing useCallback does that useMemo couldn't also express; it's a convenience wrapper for one common shape of the same underlying idea.

examples.code:
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ onItemClick }) {
  console.log("ExpensiveList rendered");
  return <div>{/* imagine thousands of rows here */}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const handleItemClick = useCallback((id) => {
    console.log("clicked", id);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Re-render Parent ({count})</button>
      <ExpensiveList onItemClick={handleItemClick} />
    </div>
  );
}

