sections.body:
Consider a `theme` value needed by a deeply nested `ThemedButton`, several layers below the component that actually owns it:

```jsx
function Page({ theme }) {
  return <Sidebar theme={theme} />;
}
function Sidebar({ theme }) {
  return <Panel theme={theme} />;
}
function Panel({ theme }) {
  return <ThemedButton theme={theme} />;
}
function ThemedButton({ theme }) {
  return <button className={theme}>Click</button>;
}
```

`Sidebar` and `Panel` both have to accept a `theme` prop and immediately forward it, purely so it can reach `ThemedButton` -- neither of them actually uses `theme` for anything themselves. This is **prop drilling**, and it gets worse the deeper or wider the tree gets: every intermediate component's prop signature becomes coupled to something that's really global configuration, not something specific to that component's own job, and adding a new consumer deep in the tree, or restructuring the tree at all, means touching every layer in between just to keep threading the value through.

```jsx
const ThemeContext = createContext("light");

function Page() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      <Sidebar />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}
function Sidebar() {
  return <Panel />;
}
function Panel() {
  return <ThemedButton />;
}
function ThemedButton() {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
  return <button className={theme}>Click</button>;
}
```

`createContext(defaultValue)` creates a Context object. Wrapping part of the tree in `<ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">` makes that value available to any descendant, no matter how deeply nested, without threading it through every component in between. `useContext(ThemeContext)`, called inside any descendant, reads the value from the nearest enclosing Provider above it. Notice `Sidebar` and `Panel` no longer mention theme at all -- they're no longer coupled to a value they never used.

```jsx
const AppContext = createContext(null);

function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: "Amara" });
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState("light");
  const value = { user, theme, setUser, setTheme };

  return (
    <AppContext.Provider value={value}>
      <ThemeLabel />
      <UserGreeting />
    </AppContext.Provider>
  );
}

function ThemeLabel() {
  const { theme } = useContext(AppContext);
  return <span>{theme}</span>;
}
function UserGreeting() {
  const { user } = useContext(AppContext);
  return <span>Hi, {user.name}</span>;
}
```

Calling `setTheme` here re-renders `App`, which builds a brand-new `value` object -- and because `AppContext`'s value reference has changed, **every** component that calls `useContext(AppContext)` re-renders, including `UserGreeting`, even though `user` never changed at all. `useContext` subscribes a component to the context's value as a single, whole unit; React has no way to know that `UserGreeting` only actually reads the `user` field and doesn't care about `theme` -- it only tracks whether the value reference as a whole changed.

